finding.evidence:
```ts
try {
  const open = (await import('open')).default;
  await open(oauthUrl);
  opened = true;
} catch { /* fall through and print the URL */ }
if (!opened) { console.error('Could not open a browser. ...'); console.error(oauthUrl); }
```
`open` v11 (packages/cli/package.json:28) resolves as soon as the child process is *spawned* when `wait` is not set — it does not report whether a browser actually launched. On a headless box, SSH session, container or CI runner, `xdg-open` usually exists and exits non-zero (or does nothing) asynchronously, so `open()` resolves, `opened` becomes `true`, and the URL is never printed. The previous code printed the URL unconditionally before attempting `open`. Net effect: `wafflebase login` prints only "Opening browser for GitHub login...", hangs for the 30 s callback timeout, and gives the user no way to complete the flow. Related: without a `wait` option, `open` does not attach an `error` listener to the subprocess, so a spawn `ENOENT` is emitted asynchronously on the ChildProcess *after* `open()` already resolved — it escapes the `try/catch` entirely and surfaces as an uncaught exception rather than the `!opened` fallback.
